  "answer_text": "Despite his name, the character Flounder from Disney's *The Little Mermaid* is **not actually a flounder** (which is a flat, brown flatfish that camouflages against the muddy ocean floor). \n\nHis design is actually based on two different types of tropical fish depending on which version of the movie you look at:\n\n### 1. The Original Animated Film (1989)\nIn the animated classic, Flounder's exact species is never officially stated in the movie. He is depicted as a chubby, bright yellow fish with teal/blue stripes and a round body. Marine biologists and fans have traditionally agreed that he was most likely modeled after a stylized **tropical reef fish**, with a **blue angelfish** being the closest match due to his vibrant coloration and rounded fins.\n\n### 2. The Live-Action Remake (2023)\nFor the photorealistic 2023 live-action film, Disney officially based Flounder's design on a **sergeant major fish** (*Abudefduf saxatilis*). \n* **About the fish:** The sergeant major is a tropical species of **damselfish**. In real life, they feature yellow upper bodies, silver-blue flanks, and five prominent vertical dark stripes. \n* **The redesign:** Because real sergeant major fish are thin and vertically compressed, the live-action CGI Flounder appeared much thinner and flatter than his chubby 1989 cartoon counterpart, which sparked a great deal of humor and debate among fans on social media when the film's posters were released!",
